    "body": "<p>The ocean soundscape is dominated by water itself and by the animals that live in it. Waves breaking on shores, wind on the surface, and rain on the sea make a broad geophony, while snapping shrimp crackle over reefs, fish grunt and drum, and whales and dolphins call, click, and sing across great distances. Sound travels about four and a half times faster in seawater than in air, and low frequencies travel furthest, so the ocean is a long-distance acoustic world in a way the land is not. Depth matters: the deep sound channel, a layer where sound speed is at a minimum, can trap low-frequency sound and carry it across ocean basins. Human shipping adds a low-frequency anthrophony layer to many ocean soundscapes, and this record notes it as a separate layer rather than folding it into the natural ocean sound.</p><h4>Science proof</h4><p>Science proof: hydrophone recordings document the ocean soundscape&#x27;s components directly: wave and wind noise spectra, snapping shrimp crackle, fish choruses at dawn and dusk, and whale calls localised by arrays. The deep sound channel, or SOFAR channel, is a measured physical feature of ocean sound-speed profiles, confirmed by long-range transmission experiments. Ocean soundscape monitoring is now used to track both marine life and human noise.</p><h4>Math proof</h4><p>Math proof: sound in seawater travels at roughly 1500 metres per second, about 1500 / 343 = 4.37 times the speed in air. A sound crossing 150 kilometres of ocean takes 150000 / 1500 = 100.0 seconds, while the same distance in air would take 150000 / 343 = 437.3 seconds. The demo prints the speed ratio 4.37, ocean travel time 100.0 seconds, and air travel time 437.3 seconds.</p><h4>Soundscape layer(s)</h4><p>biophony; geophony</p><h4>Source</h4><p>Published ocean soundscape and marine bioacoustics literature (SOFAR channel; hydrophone surveys); figures as published October 2026</p>",
